ly giving sort of 10  pounds 20 pounds here and there and  saying I'm sorry it can't be more but  it's incredibly touching to see how  willing people are to get involved yeah  and and your initial target was 10,000  pounds wasn't it you've more than  trebled that so far and that figure  rising all at the time what difference  can that money make well you know I'm  hearing from people saying things like  you know not only have they been  evacuated from their homes but their  cars obviously have been taken away  their people who still owe money on  their cars and hoping that they'll  they'll get them written off so they can  pay the finance you know the way people  who had their entire home contents in  storage at the time and the storage  places as has just been wiped out so  people you know a lot of people not  insured as well so the effects of what's  happened are gonna go on for a long long  time way beyond and who's this right now  what we're seeing it you know in the  papers people are gonna be dealing with  the effects of this a long long time so  we need to make sure that people are  looked after the people of who want  people to live in their homes have  temporary living and are able to go back  and to have money there to to replace  what they've lost and we need sure the  community services kept up and and there  for people and to help those people on  the front lines who were trying to do  something to help the recovery so that's  what all the money will go towards is  the estimation of the people who are  being affected most and the ones who are  trying to help them the most get that  support
 

